Reproduction
------------
Use the fixed affine FEM export described in nvf-affine-convergence.md.
For the core trace, first call projected_matrices(coefficients[:8],8),
then use solve_2d with the same public solver order_kxy arrays, exterior
indices 1 and 1.5, theta=.2, phi=0, incidence=(0,1), wavelength=900,
and thickness=[300]. Start tracemalloc after preparing those inputs,
record get_traced_memory immediately after the call, then stop tracing.
Run both amplitude and field declarations against the parent and current
core functions, with garbage collection before each measured call.

For the process measurement, load the same coefficients and start timing
before projected_matrices(coefficients[:8],20). Construct wavebit.rcwa
with backend=0, lattice=700*[[1,.45],[0,.7]], wavelength=900, thickness=[300],
theta=.2, phi=0, truncation_order=(20,20), top=1 and bot=2.25. Call
solve_conv with the five returned matrices each expanded to one layer.
Stop timing after the solve, save each p_inc/s_inc R/T array and record
resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss. On this macOS host
that value is bytes. The record compares all orders, not only totals.
Both NumPy and SciPy use Apple Accelerate; OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 and
OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 were set, which does not establish its actual thread count.
No package was installed, upgraded or copied for this change.
All numerical study and test processes ended.

